---
import AppShell from '../layouts/AppShell.astro';
import { AUTHOR, PROFILE_IMAGE_URL, WEBSITE_REF } from '../lib/seo';

/* The career arc as a sequence. Each step is where the next one came from, which is the whole
   point of the page: none of it was a plan, but none of it was wasted either. */
const TIMELINE = [
  { k: 'Information Technology', d: 'College in the Philippines. Graduated top of my class. Java, C++, JavaScript, HTML, and Android, back when I thought programming was the whole job.' },
  { k: 'Graphic design', d: 'Logos, flyers, t-shirts. I competed on 99designs and won hundreds of contests. Losing one teaches you faster than any course does.' },
  { k: 'Engineering company', d: 'Built and ran their website, then their marketing and SEO. First time I watched design and code turn into revenue for someone else.' },
  { k: 'Paid ads and marketing', d: 'Facebook ads, funnels, the whole demand side. I stopped thinking of myself as someone who just makes things look good.' },
  { k: 'CRM and automation', d: 'GoHighLevel, Zapier, n8n. I fell for making busywork disappear, which is really programming again wearing a different hat.' },
  { k: 'AI', d: 'Claude Code and Codex, every day. Where all of it compounds: the engineering, the design eye, and knowing what a business actually needs.' },
  { k: 'New Jersey', d: 'Where I live and build now. Still learning something most days, still in the gym to keep my head straight.' },
];

const canonical = 'https://busqueneil.com/story';
const profile = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'ProfilePage',
  '@id': `${canonical}#webpage`,
  name: 'About Neil Busque',
  url: canonical,
  isPartOf: WEBSITE_REF,
  dateCreated: '2026-05-13',
  dateModified: '2026-07-25',
  mainEntity: {
    ...AUTHOR,
    image: PROFILE_IMAGE_URL,
    jobTitle: 'AI Engineer and Product Builder',
    sameAs: ['https://www.linkedin.com/in/neilbusque/'],
  },
};
---
<AppShell
  title="About Neil Busque | Design, Engineering and AI"
  description="Who I am, what I build, and how I work. Design, programming, engineering and AI in one person, based in New Jersey and building for small teams."
  canonical={canonical}
  active="story"
>
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(profile)} />
  <span class="eyebrow">About me</span>
  <h1 class="sec">Design, engineering, and the part in <span class="mark-hl">between.</span></h1>
  <p class="story-lede">I am Neil Busque. I build web apps, websites, funnels, and automations for small teams, from New Jersey. One person, start to finish, no handoffs.</p>

  {/* Photo slot. Swap the src for a real portrait when Neil has one he likes; the figure and
      caption stay as they are. Keep it 4:5 — the CSS crops to that ratio. */}
  <figure class="about-photo">
    <img src="/assets/art/neil-avatar.webp" alt="Neil Busque" width="900" height="1125" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption>The guy who actually builds it, with my AI assistants. No account manager in between.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <article class="article-page" style="padding-top:0">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Who I am</h2>
      <p>I am a builder who came at it from three directions instead of one. I studied Information Technology and graduated top of my class. Then I spent years in graphic design, winning hundreds of contests on 99designs. Then marketing, paid ads, funnels, CRM, and automation, because the work kept asking for one more thing I did not have yet.</p>
      <p>Most people in this field have one of those. I have all three, and the combination is the whole point: I can design the thing, build the thing, and understand why the business needed it in the first place. The handoffs where projects usually lose their shape do not exist when it is one person carrying all three.</p>

      <h2>How I try to get better</h2>
      <p>I read constantly and I follow people who are genuinely better than me rather than people who are good at sounding like it. When something I built breaks, I write down why, and that note is worth more than any course I have taken.</p>
      <p>I am not interested in being adequate at this. I want the work to be the best version of itself, and that means shipping a lot, looking hard at what came out, and being honest when it is not there yet. More than 40 products this year is not a brag about volume; it is how I keep the feedback loop short enough to actually learn from.</p>
      <p>The gym is part of the same thing. It is the one habit I have never negotiated with, and it keeps my head straight when a build is going badly.</p>

      <h2>What I actually like doing</h2>
      <p>The first working version. Not the polish, not the planning document, the moment an idea stops being a conversation and turns into a URL you can open.</p>
      <p>I also like problems where the answer is not more software. A lot of what I get asked to build should not be built, and finding that out on the first call is more satisfying to me than taking the money and building it anyway.</p>
      <p>And the unglamorous half: the migration, the permissions model, the reason a form works on your phone but not your client's. Most people find that boring. It is usually where the actual difficulty lives.</p>

      <h2>How I can help you</h2>
      <p>Concretely, five things: <a href="/services/web-apps">web apps</a>, <a href="/services/websites">websites</a>, <a href="/services/landing-pages">landing pages</a>, <a href="/services/funnels">funnels</a>, and <a href="/services/ai-automation-services">automation</a>.</p>
      <p>Underneath those it is usually one of three jobs. You have a manual process eating hours a week that should be a system. You have traffic that is not converting and nobody can say which step is leaking. Or you have an idea and need someone who can take it from a sentence to a working product without a six-month discovery phase.</p>
      <p>Most first versions are live in days. You own the code and the accounts at the end, always.</p>

      <h2>How I work</h2>
      <p>I do not have an agency behind me and I am not building one. You get me on the first call, me building it, and my phone number.</p>
      <p>Scope before build, so you know what is included and what is not. Progress you can watch on a real URL instead of a status update. And if I am not the right fit, I will tell you on the first call and it costs you nothing.</p>
      <p>I lean on AI hard and stay the engineer. I use the AI app builders myself and they are good for a simple app, but generating a screen and keeping a whole system dependable are different jobs. The first version comes fast, then a real edge case appears, one fix breaks something else, and nobody is quite sure what changed. That is the part I am there for.</p>

      <h2>How I spend my time</h2>
      <p>Most of the week is client work and my own products, in Claude Code and Codex, on Eastern time.</p>
      <p>The rest goes to my wife. She is still in the Philippines, so a lot of my week is timed around an eleven-hour difference, and a fair amount of what I have built exists because of that gap. Tandem started as an app for the two of us. Watch Together did too.</p>
      <p>When I am not building for someone else, I am building something small for myself and writing down what broke. That is where <a href="/free">the free tools</a> and every guide came from.</p>

      <h2>Why I am here at all</h2>
      <p>I am from the Philippines. I live in New Jersey because of my mom.</p>
      <p>I was in high school when she left. Wages back home are low, and the math on a normal life often does not work no matter how hard you run at it. So she went to the United States and worked, alone, so the rest of us would get a shot.</p>
      <p>It took her more than ten years. She brought us over one at a time. My brothers, then my dad, then me, a few years ago. I know what that decade cost her. My wife is still in the Philippines, so I go back when I can, and getting her here is next.</p>
      <p>That is what the work is for.</p>
    </div>
  </article>

  <h2 class="sec" style="font-size:clamp(22px,2.6vw,30px); margin:var(--gap-sec) 0 18px;">The order it happened in.</h2>
  <div class="timeline">
    {TIMELINE.map((t, i) => (
      <div class="tl-row">
        <div class="tl-marker"><span class="tl-dot"></span>{i < TIMELINE.length - 1 && <span class="tl-line"></span>}</div>
        <div class="tl-body v8-card"><h3>{t.k}</h3><p>{t.d}</p></div>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <section class="hsec v8-card why-card">
    <h2>When you hire me, you get <span class="mark-hl">me.</span></h2>
    <p>Not an agency that hands you to a junior account manager. You talk to the person actually building your product, and I keep working until you are proud of it. If I am not the right fit, I will tell you on the first call and it costs you nothing.</p>
    <div class="hero-ctas" style="margin-top:18px">
      <a class="btn-sun" href="https://book.busqueneil.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Book a call</a>
      <a class="btn-ghost" href="/work">See what I've built</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</AppShell>
